# Line endings: LF everywhere, including Windows working copies.
#
# Git for Windows ships core.autocrlf=true as a system default, so without
# this every Windows checkout gets CRLF. Several things in this repo read
# their own source files back and match on exact text, and CRLF breaks all
# of them in ways that look like tool bugs rather than config:
#
#   - tools/copy-ledger writeback is exact-match-or-refuse: each occurrence
#     stores the verbatim source substring and copy-apply finds that text or
#     changes nothing. Every multi-line string misses on a CRLF checkout.
#     Seen for real on 2026-08-13 — 30 of 37 rewrites blocked with "source
#     drifted", and the 7 that landed were the single-line ones.
#   - tools/copy-ledger/drafts.js parses draft blocks with a marker regex
#     anchored on a bare LF. A CRLF draft matches zero blocks, so copy-pull
#     says "No changes found" and drops a whole writing session silently.
#   - The frontend suite asserts against source text (there is no Svelte
#     render library here), so a stray CR is a failing test with a confusing
#     message.
#
# This is a no-op for content: every one of the repo's tracked blobs is
# already LF, and the repo has no binary files and no .bat/.cmd/.ps1 that
# would want CRLF. All this changes is what lands in the working copy.
#
# Prefer this over listing the file types copy-ledger happens to touch
# today. The ledger already spans .svelte, .go, .md and .js, and the day
# copy moves into a type nobody remembered to list, the failure comes back
# wearing the same misleading costume.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The two that bled, kept explicit. `text` is unconditional where
# `text=auto` defers to git's binary heuristic, and a wrong guess on either
# of these is expensive.

# Shell scripts must stay LF even on Windows checkouts (core.autocrlf=true
# would give them CRLF, which breaks bash — including running the smoke test
# through WSL).
*.sh text eol=lf

# The copy ledger is written by tools/copy-ledger, which emits LF. On a
# core.autocrlf=true checkout git hands it back CRLF, so every run leaves
# the file "modified" with an empty diff — noise at exactly the moment
# copy-apply asks you to read a diff.
copy/ledger.json text eol=lf
